erties reported in Table 1, and considering that coolant pressure in the regenerative cooling system must be greater than pressure in the engine combustion chamber to allow propellant feeding beyond the cooling system, it is evident that in many cases the coolant will be at supercritical pressure, while it may happen that coolant will operate in the cooling system at a pressure close to the critical one. Once the steady state design condition is reached, hydrogen will always be at supercritical pressure, hydrocarbons and oxygen will be at supercritical pressure for booster/first stage engines, whereas storable propellants like hydrazines and nitrogen oxides will only be at supercritical pressure for very high pressure engines. Let us therefore consider that common values of coolant pressure at the entrance of the cooling system can be assumed to lie in the range
