 related to an abrupt change of flow density with temperature, indicating the presence of a pseudo-gas layer featuring low thermal conductivity. Looking at Fig. 4, and considering that both density and thermal conductivity variations through the Widom line soften at high reduced pressures, Fig. 5, it can be promptly seen that such a heat transfer deterioration will more likely occur in near-critical conditions rather than far from the critical pressure.
2.2. Fluid dynamic analysis
As discussed above, in the near-critical region, the effectiveness of heat transfer to a fluid is significantly influenced by the amount of entering heat flux. Differently from pool boiling, in forced con
vection, heat transfer is influenced by the ratio of entering heat flux to mass flux, qw/G, similarly to the subcritical region. In particular, at relatively high mass flux or low heat flux the heat transfer may be enhanced, while at relatively high heat flux or low mass flux a heat transfer deterioration may occur. In this section a discussion of why these quantities play a role in heated supercritical flow in channels is presented. The heat transfer deterioration phenomenon is originated, as in the pool boiling, by the formation of a low-density low-conduction layer at wall. However, when considering a turbulent flow in a tube, turbulent diffusion plays a decisive role in determining the possible occurrence of heat transfer deterioration. Turbulent diffusion has therefore to be studied in detail. In pursuing this study, it is useful to consider that, as the typical flow conditions in rocket engine cooling channels are relevant to relatively high mass flux, of the order of thousands of kg/s/m2, the resulting turbulent flow can be considered unaffected by any buoyancy effect. To investigate the flow structure under heat transfer deterioration, the turbulent flow inside a heated tube is considered to be composed of two regions [16,17]: the tiny viscous sublayer close to the wall where the turbulent shear stress t is negligible and the above turbulent core of the flow where the viscous shear stress v is negligible. According to this modeling, the heat transfer coefficient of the flow can be expressed as hc = 1/(Rv + Rt ) where Rv and Rt are the thermal resistances of the two regions. For the heat transfer deterioration to occur a sufficiently large increase of Rv and/or Rt should take place. The thermal resistance of the viscous sublayer can be estimated as:
