3. Prediction of heat transfer deterioration phenomena in straight tubes
One important aspect in designing cooling channels to be fed by a near-critical coolant is the preliminary evaluation of the possible occurrence of heat transfer deterioration. Of course, similarly to film-boiling, also heat transfer deterioration is to be avoided, because the corresponding wall temperature increase may lead to wall material failure. To better focus on the possible problem let us consider which are the necessary conditions for the phenomenon to occur. For heat transfer deterioration to occur the pseudo-gas phase must develop on the surface. This is possible if wall temperature is greater than the pseudo-critical temperature. Moreover, obviously the coolant bulk temperature has to be lower than the pseudocritical temperature else we would be in the gas-like regime. The condition that the pseudo-critical temperature is between the wall and bulk temperature is a necessary but not sufficient condition because, as underlined before, the coolant pressure as well as the heating rate will determine if heat transfer deterioration takes place. To determine if the heat transfer process can be considered deteriorated or not it is necessary to identify a deterministic criterion for its occurrence. Different criteria can be found in the literature and exhaustive reviews of them have been recently published [22,23]. In the literature, the criterion often refers to the normal heat transfer conditions and identifies a deteriorated heat transfer as that occurring at a lower rate than in normal conditions. The uncertainty of this definition is that it assumes as known the normal heat transfer coefficient value in the conditions where heat transfer deterioration is to occur. Often the normal heat transfer evaluation is arbitrarily based on the Dittus-Boelter relation. On the other hand, it is important to identify the possible occurrence
