4. Evaluation of heat transfer when deterioration phenomena occur
Once the conditions that trigger heat transfer deterioration in supercritical fluids have been identified, it is useful to derive appropriate correlations for the Nusselt number in order to evaluate crucial parameters like wall temperature and heat flux quickly, at least as far as it can be accepted to consider devices operating at mildly deteriorated conditions. Correlations for quick evaluation are particularly useful during design phase, when massive parametric analysis are required. Nusselt number correlations have been usually determined on the basis of experimental data. However, with today capabilities, they can also be calibrated on the basis of numerical simulations. As an example we report here results from such an approach which has been used in [18] to derive a Nusselt number correlation for supercritical methane in straight tubes with round section under heat transfer deterioration and with negligible buoyancy effects. In that approach the numerical solutions of the Reynolds averaged Navier–Stokes equations have been efficiently used to investigate trends of the heat transfer to supercritical fluids under a sufficiently wide range of operative conditions, circumventing the intrinsic difficulty of the experimental apparatus in exploring the same wide range of operative conditions (in terms of mass flux, heat flux, fluid pressure and temperature, etc.). The result achieved in [18] is summarized by the correlation:
