6. Rough wall channels
One important effect that has been found of major importance in determining the behavior of near critical coolants under heating and the possible occurrence of heat transfer deterioration is that of wall roughness. The role of wall roughness on heat transfer enhancement has been described by suitable correlations in normal conditions, showing that it increases with roughness following the increase of wall friction [29], thanks to the increased turbulence production with surface roughness. However, its increase tends to be less than friction increase up to flatten out in the highest range of roughness. To evaluate the effect of the wall surface roughness on the heat transfer deterioration, the same flow conditions of the rectangular cross section channel of Section 5 have been simulated for rectangular wall of increasing roughness from smooth channel to relative roughnesses of ε/Dh = 0.003 and ε/Dh = 0.0067, where ε is the absolute equivalent sand grain roughness. The results obtained at the two reduced pressures of pi/pc = 1.27 and pi/pc = 2.25 show the expected significant influence of roughness on the evolution of wall temperature along the channel (Fig. 21). At the higher reduced pressure, as shown in Section 5, no heat transfer deterioration takes place and the increasing roughness yields the expected result of increasing the convective heat transfer and therefore it yields a significant reduction of the wall temperature. On the other hand, at the lower reduced pressure, heat transfer deterioration occurs in case of smooth walls. Also in this case, the reduction of wall temperature because of the increased convective heat exchange yielded by wall roughness is quite evident from the figure. As a consequence, also heat transfer deterioration evolution is affected by wall roughness. The peak heat flux is reduced and moves towards higher fluid bulk temperatures. At the highest roughness considered the peak barely vanishes, so that heat transfer deterioration no longer occurs. The temperature evolution becomes similar to that seen at higher pressure, with the smooth temperature increase which is anticipated, with respect to the higher pressure case, because of the lower pseudo-critical temperature. In fact, the pseudo-critical temperature is T/Tc = 1.03 at p/pc = 1.27, and T/Tc = 1.14 at p/pc = 2.25.
These results show that wall roughness has a mitigating effect with respect to heat transfer deterioration especially when high roughness levels are considered, and therefore that criteria devel
