7. Conclusions
An overview of the importance of the possible occurrence of heat transfer deterioration in cooling channels of liquid rocket engines which use light hydrocarbons as the fuels, has been presented. In particular, the achievements of the Authors and their coworkers during the last decade have been summarized. The present review shows that heat transfer deterioration while heating supercritical liquid rocket engine propellants may occur under specific conditions. In particular, it is seen that the high level of heat flux typical of liquid rocket engines can lead to values of heat-to-mass flux ratio that trigger this undesired phenomenon. On the other hand, it has been discussed and highlighted that its possible occurrence is mitigated in case of pressure much higher than critical and by wall roughness. The models for a first evaluation of the required condition for the onset of the heat transfer deterioration and the wall temperature evolution developed for methane and light hydrocarbons have been shown as able to provide conservative predictions. This is because they are based on the case of smooth and uniformly heated tubes which are more prone to deteriorate than the actual rough asymmetrically heated channels. These models pave the way for the possible future refinements able to include the role of asymmetry and wall roughness.
